statement:
Let $p, q, b \in \bbr$ satisfy $1 \le p \le q \le 2$ and $0 \le b \le 1$, and set $\rho = \sqrt{(p-1)/(q-1)}$. Then
\[
  \frac{(1+\rho b)^{q} + (1-\rho b)^{q}}{2}
  \;\le\;
  \left(\frac{(1+b)^{p} + (1-b)^{p}}{2}\right)^{q/p}.
\]

replaced:
Let $1 \le p \le q \le 2$, $b \in [0,1]$ and $\rho = \sqrt{(p-1)/(q-1)}$. Then
\[
  \frac{(1+\rho b)^q + (1-\rho b)^q}{2}
  \;\le\;
  \Bigl(\frac{(1+b)^p + (1-b)^p}{2}\Bigr)^{q/p}.
\]
